ladies and gentlemen it's absolutely  wonderful to be here today you'll  probably be able to detect from my  accent that I spent much of my childhood  growing up in Africa and one of the  things which I love about Africa one of  the many things which I love about  Africa is Africans they love to tell  stories and if you think about it that's  how we have communicated since the  beginning of time that's how we have  connected since the beginning of time  it's by telling stories and so what I  want to do today is I want to tell you  three short stories three short stories  from three places which I know very very  well and which I think are going to have  a very big impact on every single person  in this room so would you come with me  now because I want to take you to the  first place I want to take you high up  into the Arctic to a place called  Spitsbergen now Spitsbergen is on the  edge of the Arctic sea Isis in the  Norwegian Arctic back in 2005 I went to  the Norwegian Arctic to undertake my  first very very cold  long-distance swim in 2005 the water  temperature there was 3 degrees  centigrade I went back there last year  and the water was no longer 3 degrees  centigrade the water was now 10 degrees  centigrade so the water had gone from 3  degrees to 10 degrees in just 12 years  and what scientists are telling us now  is very very clear what they are saying  is that within the next 12 years  they're expecting there to be very  little sea ice left in the Arctic in the  summer months so if you were a child  entering school right now when you leave  school in 12 years time you're going to  be entering a world which is very very  different to the world in which you  started your schooling right now what is  happening in the Arctic what is  happening to the north of us is going to  be impacting every single person on this  planet every single future generation  and the whole of  the animal kingdom let me take you now  from the Arctic all the way down to the  bottom of the world down to Antarctica  on the edge of Antarctica is an  incredible group of islands  it's called South Sandwich Islands and  when you arrive there it's like you have  arrived literally into a Polar Garden of  Eden over all the beaches are these  amazing penguins thousands and thousands  of penguins in the water these whales  coming up out of the sea and gorging  krill and you'll also see these enormous  great elephant seals some of them as big  as 3 tons coming out of the water  lumbering out of the water in order to  fight in order to mate ladies and  gentlemen there's a reason why Sir David  Attenborough covered the South Sandwich  Islands with so much detail in Blue  Planet 2 and the reason is because these  islands are unique and they are very  very precious then we leave the South  Sandwich Islands and then we come back  here to the United Kingdom back here to  the English Channel so I've just done  the swim from Dover I mean from Lands  End  all the way to Dover it was an enormous  swim it took me 49 days it was a  toughest swim I've ever done in my whole  life I started at Lands End and then I  swam from Lands End  all the way along the Cornish beaches  then to Plymouth where I grew up as a  young boy accross slime Bay around the  bottom of the Isle of whites past the  Seven Sisters around Dungeness and then  finally finally the Seven Sisters and  then finally the White Cliffs of Dover  it took me 49 days and what struck me  about this swim was two things the first  thing that struck me was just how  incredibly beautiful the coastline is  here in the United Kingdom it's  incredibly beautiful but the second  thing was struck me was how little  wildlife I saw I saw so many jellyfish I  mean they're a function of the fact now  that our waters are warming and also the  facts  that there now is so much overfishing so  their numbers of blooming I had the  jellyfish sting me in the face  underneath the armpits in the groin  absolutely everywhere okay but the other  thing was that I just saw a few Birds  I saw a few dolphins in Lyme Bay  I saw one turtle one turtle of Portland  and very little else and that was in 49  days and every beach I went to every  single beach I just saw this plastic  everywhere it's like we've hoovered up  the fish and we thrown in the plastic  and that is why it is so exciting that  the announcement that the United Kingdom  made last week now there was a lot of  stuff happening last week so you may  have missed it but the United Kingdom  made this announcement that they are  calling on all nations in the world all  nations to come together now to protect  at least 30 percent of our oceans now to  protect it by 2030 this is a huge  breakthrough I've come here today to  thank you very very much but there's a  rub to this and that is that you also  have to get your own house in order it  will be impossible for the United  Kingdom to lead on ocean conservation  which I think it should do but it'll be  impossible to do it unless you also have  your own home in order around the United  Kingdom in the waters around the United  Kingdom you have only fully protected  just seven square kilometres I mean just  think about it I mean it's shocking said  only seven square kilometers of the  waters around the United Kingdom are  fully protected let me just give you an  example of how this plays out at the end  of the English Channel is an incredible  iconic landmark it's called Goodwin  Sands and on Goodwin Sands this is where  all the seals come up this is a place  where they're endangered eels and lots  of birds and so your government has set  this area side now as a new potential  what you call a marine conservation zone  but at the same time you also got plans  to allow the Dover Harbour Board to come  along and to dredge this area to get  aggregate and sand in order for them to  build their new Harbor development so on  the one hand you want to protect this  place you want to build this incredible  marine conservation zone and yet on the  other hand you want to dig it up  protection must mean protection and the  same goes for your overseas territories  I talked about the South Sandwich  Islands which are just so very very  amazing they are incredible places they  are one of the most important  biodiversity hotspots on the entire  planet and yet less than 2% of the South  Sandwich Islands and the neighboring  South Georgia are actually fully  protected two percent I urge you to  fully protect the South Sandwich Islands  with just one stroke of the pen these  these islands are under British  jurisdiction with just one stroke of the  pen you could create a protected area  right now which has double the size of  the United Kingdom please you have to  stop thinking that you can sustainably  harvest an area that you can sustainably  manage an area and at the same time you  can also protect it you can't Britain I  urge you to set this area aside this  should be your gift to the world I now  want to end off by bringing this story  back here to this room to this  conference center to you here in  Birmingham what are all these stories  about ladies and gentlemen it is very  very clear to me that we have now  entered the era of big consequences our  environment is changing very very  quickly our environment now is right on  the edge Desmond Tutu a man which I love  who I love so very much  he always used to say that many of the  conflicts around the world are simply  conflicts over limited resources  when we damage our environments we  create conditions which are ripe for  conflict but when we protect our  environments we actually foster peace so  so many people come up to me and they  say well what can i as one person do to  try and save the environment well it's  very very clear that not one person can  fix the environment not one political  party can fix the environment and not  one country can fix the environments the  only way we're going to be able to solve  this problem is if all of us work  together the only way we're going to be  able to solve this problem is if we are  all talking to each other the only way  we're going to be able to solve this  problem is if we all build bridges I  urge you to do this this can be our  generations legacy ladies and gentlemen  thank you all very very much  [Applause]
 

